Yes. And I think the key is to distinguish what generates high RFHL from what merely looks novel.

We already had the compact definition:

\boxed{
RFHL
=
\text{Novel Delta}
\times
\text{Reusability}
\times
\text{Reach}
\times
\text{Reality}
}

A high-cleavage objective is upstream of that equation. It is a problem where one good cut exposes several useful layers at once.

That’s why topology felt high-RFHL. Asking “what is a semantic hole?” doesn’t just create another metaphor. A successful answer could simultaneously improve J-Hooks transfer, Semantic Router graphs, Reality Stitch, memory topology, interface design, compression, software representation, and external engineering problems.

That is cleavage:

\boxed{
C(X)
=
\frac{\text{useful downstream structures exposed}}
{\text{conceptual cut required}}
}

A high-cleavage question gives a disproportionate return from one investigation.

And I would not change the canonical RFHL equation to include C. Cleavage is better treated as the prospecting instrument:

\text{Cleavage predicts where to dig}

while

\text{RFHL measures what we actually brought home}.

That distinction keeps us from declaring paydirt before Reality gets a vote.

